A. the 3-adic clock tower
   n=1: L = ord_(3^1)(2) = 2; v_3(2^L - 1) = 1; new primes []
   n=2: L = ord_(3^2)(2) = 6; v_3(2^L - 1) = 2; new primes [7]
   n=3: L = ord_(3^3)(2) = 18; v_3(2^L - 1) = 3; new primes [19, 73]
   n=4: L = ord_(3^4)(2) = 54; v_3(2^L - 1) = 4; new primes [87211, 262657]
   n=5: L = ord_(3^5)(2) = 162; v_3(2^L - 1) = 5; new primes [163, 2593, 71119, 135433, 97685839, 272010961]
  ok   ord_(3^n)(2) = 2*3^(n-1) for n <= 7 (2 is a primitive root mod 3^n)
  ok   v_3(2^(2*3^(n-1)) - 1) = n (lifting the exponent)
  ok   63 = 9*7 brings 7; 2^18 - 1 = 27*7*19*73 brings 19 and 73
  ok   ord_19(2) = ord_27(2) = 18, ord_73(2) = 9, ord_7(2) = 3: the primes with 2-clock dividing 18 are 3, 7, 19, 73
  ok   2^a mod 27 -> 2^a mod 19 is a group isomorphism (Z/27)^* -> F_19^* (both C_18 generated by 2): one mod-18 exponent clock
  ok   the joint residue of 2^a mod 513 = 27*19 has period exactly 18
B. the trunk (4^j - 1)/3 and its dead ends
  ok   a_(j+1) = R(a_j), R(n) = 4n + 1 (the odometer / sibling ladder of 1)
  ok   a_3 = 21 = 63/3 and a_9 = 87381 = (2^18 - 1)/3 = 9*7*19*73
  ok   v_3(a_(3^k)) = k, k <= 5
  ok   a_j is a dead end (multiple of 3: no odd predecessor) iff 3 | j
C. Mersenne numbers and the backward sieve
  ok   a = 5 mod 6: 2^a - 1 = 4 mod 9, smaller ancestor (8n-5)/9 at depth 2
  ok   a = 1, 3 mod 6, a <= 120: backward-minimal except [13, 67, 69, 103]; a = 13, 67 (= 13 mod 54 = ord_81(2)) share the word (2,2,1,1)
   backward-minimal odd exponents a <= 120: 35 of 59 = 0.593  (3-adic prediction 2 s_inf in [0.5764, 0.5982])
  ok   odd a mod 2*3^(k-1) -> 2^a mod 3^k is a bijection onto the classes = 2 mod 3: Mersenne numbers sample the 3-adic classes = 1 mod 3 uniformly
D. the rewrite compiler's residual seeds are backward-minimal
  ok   all 239 residual seeds are backward-minimal: 153 multiples of 3 (no ancestors) + 86 with no smaller ancestor (depth 40)
   base rate: odd n <= 10000, 3 not dividing n, first reset 2: 834 numbers, backward-minimal fraction 0.294
